(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-ent-antihistamines-steroids-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Antihistamines & Corticosteroids Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient on a first generation antihistamine reports dry mouth, blurred vision and difficulty passing urine. What explains this?","choices":["Histamine receptor blockade itself","The antimuscarinic action of the drug","An allergic reaction to the drug","Interaction with alcohol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three complaints from three different organs, all explained by one receptor family being blocked. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What effect does histamine have on the heart through H2 receptors?","choices":["It increases contractility","It slows conduction through the atrioventricular node","It reduces contractility","It has no cardiac effect"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A cardiac action is easy to miss in a receptor filed under the stomach. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What should a patient taking a first generation antihistamine be warned about?","choices":["It must not be taken together with food","Sedation is additive with alcohol and other depressants","It raises the blood pressure","It causes rebound symptoms if stopped"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The risk is in the combination rather than the drug alone, which is what makes it easy to stumble into. Central nervous system depressants generally. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What are nasal corticosteroids indicated for?","choices":["Acute bacterial sinusitis","Allergic and vasomotor rhinitis","Otitis externa","Chronic cough"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The same two conditions the nasal antihistamine covers, which is why the two are often used together. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A patient using a nasal spray develops a nosebleed. What are they told to do?","choices":["Continue and use the other nostril only","Reduce to one spray daily and continue","Stop the spray and follow up immediately","Apply pressure and carry on as normal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is a stop-and-be-seen instruction rather than a wait-and-see one, because septal perforation is what lies beyond it. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which infection is specifically a contraindication to systemic corticosteroids?","choices":["Bacterial sinusitis","Viral upper respiratory infection","Systemic fungal infection","Otitis media"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A drug that suppresses immunity given to someone with a disseminated fungal infection makes the infection worse. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What do H1 antagonists do peripherally?","choices":["Reduce gastric acid secretion","Increase mucus secretion to clear allergens","Reduce secretions, edema, hives and itching","Cause bronchoconstriction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Each of these is the mirror image of an H1 action, which is why the antagonist's effects can be read straight off the receptor. Block smooth muscle contraction. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which agent is singled out as having the strongest antimuscarinic action, especially for motion sickness?","choices":["Doxylamine","Azelastine","Cetirizine","Promethazine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The antimuscarinic strength is what makes it the motion sickness choice, not its antihistamine potency. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A patient on digoxin is started on dexamethasone. Why does that raise the risk of arrhythmia?","choices":["Dexamethasone lowers potassium","Dexamethasone raises digoxin levels directly","Digoxin increases the effect of dexamethasone","Both prolong the QT interval independently"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The interaction runs through the electrolyte rather than between the two drugs directly, which is why checking potassium is what protects the patient. Low potassium predisposes to digoxin toxicity. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Why is a patient told not to tilt the head back when using a nasal spray?","choices":["It causes the spray to hit the septum","It increases the risk of nosebleed","It draws the medicine into the throat","It reduces absorption through the skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It wastes the dose and produces the bitter taste, which is the complaint that stops people using the spray at all. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Why does a macrolide antibiotic matter in a patient taking dexamethasone?","choices":["Macrolides reduce dexamethasone clearance","Macrolides inactivate dexamethasone","Dexamethasone blocks macrolide absorption","The two combine to cause hyperkalemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The steroid is not changed; it simply stays around longer, which is enough to turn a tolerable course into a problematic one. So its effect is amplified. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is the role of an antihistamine in anaphylaxis?","choices":["Adjuvant only","First-line treatment","It has no role at all","It replaces adrenaline in a mild case"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The word matters: it supports treatment rather than being it, and the drug that actually treats anaphylaxis is adrenaline. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What are the described side effects of nasal corticosteroids?","choices":["Nosebleed, perforation and an unpleasant taste","Glucose intolerance and fluid retention","Sedation and dry mouth","Rebound congestion on stopping"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three are local, which is the trade-off that makes a nasal steroid different from a systemic one. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT among the listed indications for H1 antagonists?","choices":["Allergic rhinitis and urticaria","Motion sickness and vestibular disturbance","An adjuvant role in anaphylaxis","Bacterial sinusitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- this is not one. The indications are allergic reactions, motion sickness and vestibular symptoms, sleep remedies, and an adjuvant role in anaphylaxis. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Through which second messenger pathway do H1 receptors raise intracellular calcium?","choices":["Adenylate cyclase, raising cyclic AMP","Direct opening of a ligand-gated sodium channel","Inhibition of guanylate cyclase","Phospholipase C, producing IP3 and DAG"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is the contrast with H2 that matters: H1 works through phospholipase C, H2 through adenylate cyclase and cyclic AMP. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What produces the flare, the redness spreading out around the original insult?","choices":["Direct vasodilation by histamine at that site","Fluid leaking from post-capillary venules","A reflex through axons causing vasodilation","Mast cells migrating outward from the site"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is a nerve reflex rather than direct spread of histamine, which is why the flare extends beyond where the histamine actually is. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which musculoskeletal harms are described with systemic corticosteroids?","choices":["Muscle cramps and myalgia only","Joint effusions and stiffness","Gout and joint crystal deposition","Tendon rupture and long bone fracture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Structural damage rather than discomfort, and it can appear without warning. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which warning accompanies the list of nasal corticosteroids?","choices":["They must not be used for longer than a few days","Many have inhaled versions for asthma","They are all prescription only","They cannot be used alongside an antihistamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The example given is fluticasone: the nasal product and the asthma inhaler are different preparations of the same drug with different names. The names are easy to confuse. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What effect do H1 receptors have on smooth muscle and airways?","choices":["They lower smooth muscle tone and cause bronchodilation","They raise smooth muscle tone and cause bronchoconstriction","They have no effect on smooth muscle","They act only on vascular smooth muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is why an H1 antagonist relaxes airway and gut smooth muscle, and why histamine itself is a bronchoconstrictor. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 29"}]};
